# tim_timer.checks - one row per CHECK of fixtures/src/tim_timer.s (D-125, plan 03-21). Columns separated by one TAB: check index (1-based, the entry number of the DCK1 table), slug, class tag, meaning.
# Class tags: MAN:<page> (the literal is a manual page value; the page label must be in fixtures/lib/cites.txt), GUEST:<model> (the expected byte is computed in the guest, the table entry is an XOR mask of $00), ASSUMED:<LED-id>.
# Rows 1 to 10 are the tracer: a timer started at reload 0 delivers a TIQ interrupt through the TIMER vector, the handler is entered with I set and D clear, the entry pushed the return address and the status byte with B = 0, the request register shows TIQ until a write clears it, and the RTI returns.
# Rows 11 to 13 read the reset state. Rows 14 to 27 read the counter after counted delays: the expected value is the reload minus the elapsed CPU cycles over 256 (the prescaler step of 3072 master clocks over 12 master clocks per CPU cycle at the low speed), written by the assembler from the instruction counts of
# the source. Every row that depends on that ratio is E2 self-consistency (ER-3): the CPU cycle length is community-reported, so these rows can only show that the core agrees with itself. Rows 28 to 33 take the interrupt with the request masked by TIQD and by I.
# Evidence (ER-3, ER-4): the MAN rows follow register and interrupt facts of the hardware manual, the GUEST rows compare cells the guest wrote itself or values the assembler computed from instruction counts, the ASSUMED rows state this project's modelling assumptions. None states a timer rate or a CPU speed
# as a fact; the one claim of a step and a period is the C-level case timer_pure_function. A pass is not a support claim.
id=10
checks=33
1	irq-taken	MAN:H8-21	A borrow with I and TIQD clear requests the CPU: the TIMER vector handler ran once (it stops the timer and acknowledges, so there is no second entry)
2	pushed-status	MAN:H8-12	The entry pushed the status byte with B = 0 and the interrupted flags: D = 1 and I = 0 as the main program had them (mask $1C, B D I)
3	handler-i-set	MAN:H8-12	I is set inside the handler
4	handler-d-clear	MAN:H8-12	D is reset inside the handler (the main program ran with D = 1)
5	req-tiq-set	MAN:H8-15	Bit 2 of the request register ($1403) reads 1 while the timer interrupt is requested
6	req-tiq-ack	MAN:H8-14	A write to the request register resets TIQ: the register read after the write has bit 2 clear
7	pushed-pch	GUEST:interrupted-loop	The pushed return address high byte is the page of the wait loop the interrupt came from
8	pushed-pcl	GUEST:interrupted-loop	The pushed return address low byte lies inside the wait loop (computed in the guest as a flag)
9	resumes-after-rti	GUEST:marker	The RTI returned to the main program, which then stored its marker $5A
10	stopped-no-reirq	MAN:H8-20	The handler stopped the timer (control bit 0 = 0) and acknowledged: six prescaler steps later there was no further interrupt
11	reset-tiq-clear	MAN:H8-18	TIQ is reset by the reset: bit 2 of the request register reads 0 before anything touches the timer
12	reset-disable-clear	MAN:H8-16	The disable register is all 0 after the reset: bits 2 to 0 of $1402 read 0
13	stopped-no-borrow	MAN:H8-18	The timer is stopped by the reset: nearly twelve prescaler steps pass and no borrow requests the interrupt
14	count-before-step	GUEST:timer-e2	E2: reload 127, 251 CPU cycles after the start (under the 256 of one step): the counter reads 127
15	count-after-step	GUEST:timer-e2	E2: 257 cycles after the start, one step has happened: 126 (the first step boundary seen from both sides together with row 14)
16	count-floors	GUEST:timer-e2	E2: 1271 cycles, four steps and 96 percent of a fifth: the counter reads 123, the steps that have happened, never rounded up
17	count-via-0c01	GUEST:timer-e2	E2: the control address reads the same counter as the reload address (D-109): 605 cycles, two steps, 125
18	period-reload-plus-one	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0002	E2: reload 2 and four steps: 2, 1, 0, the borrow reloads 2, then 1, so the period is reload + 1 = 3 steps (assumption A1)
19	borrow-reloads-not-7f	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	E2: reload 5 and six steps: the step that follows 0 borrows and the counter reads the reload value 5, with no $7F window after the borrow
20	start-loads-reload	MAN:H8-22	The counter is loaded with the reload register when control bit 0 goes from 0 to 1: 77, not the held counter and not the old reload
21	stop-holds	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	E2: a stopped timer holds its counter (127 minus the two steps counted from the instruction counts)
22	stop-holds-long	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	E2: the held counter is the same eleven steps later
23	stopped-write0-noop	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	E2: a write of 0 to a stopped timer changes nothing
24	reload-write-keeps-counter	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0002	E2: a reload write while running does not touch the counter: three steps from the start the counter reads 97, not about 19
25	running-write1-noop	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	E2: a write of 1 to a running timer neither reloads the counter nor restarts the prescaler: three steps from the start the counter reads 124, not 126
26	bit7-from-io-buffer-set	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	Bit 7 of a counter read comes from io_buffer (D-104): with io_buffer = $FF the read of the 7-bit counter $2A is $AA
27	bit7-from-io-buffer-clear	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	With io_buffer = $00 the same read is the 7-bit counter, $2A
28	latched-while-tiqd	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	The borrow sets TIQ although TIQD disables the interrupt (six borrows at reload 0): bit 2 of the request register reads 1
29	tiqd-masks-delivery	MAN:H8-16	TIQD = 1 disables the timer interrupt: with I clear and TIQ set the handler did not run
30	many-borrows-one-entry	MAN:H8-15	TIQ is one bit: six borrows give one request, so unmasking it gives one entry (the handler stops the timer and acknowledges)
31	i-masks-delivery	MAN:H8-21	The CPU is requested only if I is 0: with I set and TIQD clear the handler did not run
32	latched-while-i-set	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0003	The request was latched while I masked it: bit 2 of the request register reads 1
33	pushed-t-after-cli-set	ASSUMED:LED-IRQ-0005	With the sample using the I flag from before the instruction (irq_i_sample pre) the interrupt is not taken after CLI but after the SET that follows, and the entry pushed T = 1 (bit 5 of the status byte)
